1
00:00:01,400 --> 00:00:10,184
In the 1400s, the Inca empire sent its messages along a road system thousands of kilometres long, through the Andes.

2
00:00:11,284 --> 00:00:16,544
Runners, called chasqui, carried each message in relays, from one post to the next.

3
00:00:17,244 --> 00:00:22,240
By one estimate, a message could cover about 240 kilometres in a day.

4
00:00:22,940 --> 00:00:29,383
At every post, the runner changed. The road stayed, and so did the ability to carry a message along it.

5
00:00:30,683 --> 00:00:33,131
A capability outlasts whoever performs it.

6
00:00:40,431 --> 00:00:44,179
Tomás's new note asks what the utility must be able to do.

7
00:00:44,879 --> 00:00:48,812
His first answer copies the org chart: one box for each team.

8
00:00:49,812 --> 00:00:55,993
He puts them on the wall: Network Operations, Customer Service, Finance, Regulatory affairs.

9
00:00:56,693 --> 00:00:58,445
It looks complete. It isn't.

10
00:01:01,745 --> 00:01:04,937
He takes them to Grace, who owns network operations.

11
00:01:05,637 --> 00:01:10,035
Network Operations, she says, is a team. A team is who does the work.

12
00:01:11,235 --> 00:01:16,880
Ask what it does, and you get a list: manage outages, maintain assets, connect customers.

13
00:01:17,880 --> 00:01:22,446
Those are capabilities: what the utility must be able to do, whoever does it.

14
00:01:25,146 --> 00:01:27,921
A capability says what, not who, and not how.

15
00:01:28,621 --> 00:01:33,219
The test is one question: would it still be needed after a reorganisation?

16
00:01:34,519 --> 00:01:37,452
Manage outages would be. The team might not be.

17
00:01:38,152 --> 00:01:41,568
So name each capability as an ability, in a few words.

18
00:01:44,268 --> 00:01:50,387
The map starts with the few things the utility must always be able to do. Five of them, at level 1.

19
00:01:51,487 --> 00:01:58,014
Under each, level 2 holds the parts. Under run the network: manage outages, and maintain assets.

20
00:01:59,314 --> 00:02:06,313
Connect customers, from the same list, sits under serve customers. The team's list doesn't match the map, and it shouldn't.

21
00:02:07,513 --> 00:02:17,047
Go down to level 3 only where a rule depends on order. Restoring supply is one: a fault is found, a crew is sent, and supply comes back.

22
00:02:20,247 --> 00:02:24,547
Each capability needs an owner: the person who answers for whether it works.

23
00:02:25,247 --> 00:02:32,063
Grace owns running the network. Farah owns serving customers. Ama owns reporting to the regulator.

24
00:02:32,763 --> 00:02:37,554
One capability has no owner yet. Generating energy has no name on it.

25
00:02:38,854 --> 00:02:41,908
A capability with no owner is a gap in the map.

26
00:02:44,608 --> 00:02:49,842
Next, the heat map. Each capability is coloured by how well it works, from the evidence.

27
00:02:51,042 --> 00:02:59,011
The hottest is connecting customers. A new connection takes about thirty-five working days. The outcome from Why it moves was ten.

28
00:03:00,311 --> 00:03:04,534
A heat map shows where to look first. It doesn't say where the money should go.

29
00:03:05,634 --> 00:03:07,895
That choice is made against the principles.

30
00:03:10,595 --> 00:03:16,826
Next year the utility reorganises. Network Operations splits, and its teams move to new homes.

31
00:03:17,526 --> 00:03:20,600
The teams move. The capabilities stay where they are.

32
00:03:22,100 --> 00:03:26,049
Capabilities are the stable spine. Teams come and go beneath it.

33
00:03:29,149 --> 00:03:35,561
The map could go on forever. Each capability could be split again, into level 4, and then level 5.

34
00:03:36,261 --> 00:03:38,787
Declare how deep the map goes, and stop there.

35
00:03:39,987 --> 00:03:44,638
Splitting detect faults further would give steps that nobody decides on separately.

36
00:03:45,338 --> 00:03:49,850
Fewer, well-made boxes beat a complete catalogue that nobody reads.

37
00:03:55,550 --> 00:04:00,343
Grace confirms the first two levels. The heat map goes to the board, as a draft.

38
00:04:01,043 --> 00:04:06,930
Tomás's note has an answer: five things the utility must be able to do, each with an owner.

39
00:04:08,230 --> 00:04:10,852
Next, he asks how value reaches a customer.
